                "text": "I'm trying cell count as a possible rule, but shapes with different colors end up with the same count, so that doesn't distinguish them. Now I'm considering whether the number of bends or corners in each shape determines its color instead.\n\nLooking at examples, shapes colored 1 seem to be simple L-shapes with a single corner, while shapes colored 2 seem to have more branches or two corners, like Z/S or T-shapes. I'm going through each shape's cell coordinates in example 1 to verify this pattern holds for the 2-colored shapes.\n\nChecking the other shape confirms the T-junction pattern - I'm finding that shapes with a branch creating a T intersection get labeled 2, while shapes with just corners (U, S/Z, C forms) without any T-junction are labeled 6.\n\nThat confirms the U-shape count of 2 corners equals 6. Now checking another shape with a vertical line branching right into a T junction, which matches the value 2, and I'm starting to test a third shape's coordinates.\n\nShape 5 ends with just one corner, another L shape \u21921. Shape 6, rows11-13, also single corner, L shape \u21921. Confirming shape 3 does have that T-junction I noted earlier. Now I'm ready to assemble the final answer.",
